BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Past & Present: Lost in Translation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261204T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261204T143000
UID:past-present-lost-translation@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:Past & Present: Lost in Translation\n\nPast & Present: Lost in
  Translation Friday\, December 04\, 1:00 p.m. to 2:30 p.m. Virtual Panel v
 ia YouTube Join Onsite Gallery for a thought-provoking virtual panel featu
 ring Alize Zorlutuna\, Richa Narvekar\, Dedra McDermott and Tannis Nielsen
  exploring how artists draw on cultural traditions\, ancestral knowledge\,
  and diasporic ways of knowing to shape contemporary creative practices. T
 he discussion will focus on how visual art\, dance\, design\, storytelling
 \, and material practices can reinterpret cultural knowledge while creatin
 g new relationships to memory\, identity\, land\, material culture\, and t
 he more-than-human. The panelists will share how their practices engage wi
 th inherited traditions\, not simply as forms to preserve\, but as living 
 sources for experimentation\, reclamation\, and new ways of understanding 
 the present. Stay involved with a live Q&A session\, moderated by Onsite G
 allery’s Programs & Community Coordinator\, Susan Jama . ASL Interpretat
 ion will be available. The panel is live-streamed via YouTube Visit Regist
 ration Page About the Panelists Images (left to right): Alize Zorlutuna ph
 oto by Farihah Aliyah Shaw. Richa Narvekar Alize Zorlutuna is a queer inte
 rdisciplinary artist\, writer\, and educator whose practice explores relat
 ionships between land\, material culture\, and the more-than-human through
  diasporic ways of knowing. Moving between Tkarónto and Anatolia has cult
 ivated an attentiveness to sites of encounter\, where bodies\, geographies
 \, and elemental forces meet in ongoing processes of negotiation and co-be
 coming. Drawing on material and relational traditions from the SWANA regio
 n\, their work spans video\, installation\, ebru (Anatolian water marbling
 )\, ceramics\, textiles\, and participatory practice\, bringing together d
 iverse materials\, methods\, and geographies to consider place\, memory\, 
 and relation. Richa Narvekar is an Architectural Designer and Visual Artis
 t that is interested in the connect between Art\, Architecture\, Urban and
  Interior Design. She earned her Bachelors in Architecture at CEPT Univers
 ity\, India and was a British Council scholar to the Masters in Architectu
 re\, Urban Design program at The Bartlett School of Architecture\, London\
 , UK. She is a registered Architect under the Council of Architecture\, In
 dia and holds 15+ years of experience across India and Canada. Her practic
 e\, based out of Toronto\, Canada and Goa\, India is multidisciplinary\, w
 ith a focus on projects in Interior Design. She has led several interior d
 esign and build projects as a Senior Designer for firms in the Greater Tor
 onto Area. She is also involved with sustainable architectural projects an
 d community focused art and urban initiatives in both Canada and India. He
 r work is centered on an exploration of vernacular\, critical regionalist\
 , decolonial and anti-capitalist approaches to material and ways of making
 . Images (left to right): Dedra McDermott photo by Christine Do. Tannis Ni
 elsen and Susan Jama . Dedra McDermott is a Black artist-researcher\, cura
 tor\, producer\, movement dramaturg\, and cultural steward working at the 
 intersection of choreographic performance\, archival practice\, and visual
  culture. Following a successful career as a professional contemporary dan
 cer across stage and screen\, two significant concussions in 2018 catalyze
 d a profound pivot toward creative leadership. Since then\, Dedra has char
 ted a rapid\, wide-ranging trajectory\, navigating a nimble transition fro
 m emerging to mid-career practice. Grounded by a multifaceted academic fou
 ndation (BFA ’16\, MA ’20\, MFA ’24)\, her cross-disciplinary work c
 hampions racialized artists navigating complex creative processes. As an e
 volving arts leader\, she supports creators re-educating the public on glo
 bal Indigeneity and Black diasporic histories across Latin America and the
  Caribbean. Dedra is one of the former Co-Executive Artistic Producers of 
 DanceWorks (2023–25). During her tenure\, she curated and produced over 
 15 live presentations and choreographic development programs\, while leadi
 ng access-based initiatives for hard-of-hearing and d/Deaf audiences along
 side community partners like VibraFusionLab. As a cultural steward who val
 ues the art of gathering\, Dedra works equitably to bring a diverse public
  together—creating the conditions for deeper cultural connection and app
 reciation. Tannis Nielsen (Red River Metis and Danish ancestry) has twenty
 -seven years of professional experience in the arts\, cultural and communi
 ty sectors\, and seventeen years teaching practice at the post-secondary l
 evel. Tannis holds a Masters in Visual Studies Degree (M.V.S.- 2006) from 
 the University of Toronto\, an Art and Art History-Specialist Degree from 
 U of T\, as well as a Diploma in Art and Art History from Sheridan College
 \, in Oakville\, Ontario. Tannis’ research interests include: anti-colon
 ial/anti-capitalist theory\, Indigenous decolonization methodologies\, Ind
 igenous pedagogies/oral histories\, Indigenous feminism\, Indigenous arts 
 activism(s)\, Indigenous governance/natural law(s)\, and the relative inve
 stigations between Indigenous science and quantum physics. As a visual art
 ist Tannis’s practice includes: public art\, drawing\, painting\, new me
 dia installation\, sculpture and performance. While at OCAD-U Tannis has s
 erved as Chair on the Indigenous Education council\, the Equity and Divers
 ity Committee. The Aboriginal Engagement Committee at UBC-O and has sat as
  Advisor to the Toronto District School Board\, a member of the Toronto Na
 tive Community History Project and is the past President of The Associatio
 n for Native Development in the Performing and Visual Arts (A.N.D.P.V.A.)\
 , a national Native arts organization in service since 1972. Tannis curren
 tly teaches in the painting and drawing stream at OCAD-U About the Moderat
 or Susan Jama is an art worker with over 6 years of experience leading com
 munity engagement and public arts programming with strong grassroots exper
 ience. She is the Programs and Community Coordinator at Onsite Gallery. Su
 san has worked with various institutions that approach heritage in a commu
 nity-minded manner including Toronto Ward Museum (TWM) and Black Artists
 ’ Networks in Dialogue Gallery & Cultural Centre (BAND). She graduated w
 ith a Masters of Museum Studies from University of Toronto and completed h
 er Bachelor in Psychology & History at York University. She currently serv
 es on the Museum Education Roundtable board as co-treasurer.
LOCATION:Online-Only Virtual via YouTube
URL:https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mj5JJ3zPakc
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
