BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Exhibition Opening: Tectonic Shifts
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261203T170036
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261203T210038
UID:exhibition-opening-tectonic-shifts@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:The first exhibition co-curated by Contemporary Calgary curato
 rs Mona Filip\, Kanika Anand\, and Muriel N. Kahwagi\, Tectonic Shifts inv
 ites viewers to consider the subterranean forces that shape\, uphold\, or 
 unsettle inherited structures.\n\nExhibition Opening Tectonic Shifts *Artw
 ork Credit: Caroline Monnet. Pidikwe (still)\, 2025. Courtesy of the artis
 t. Michael Belmore\, Millie Chen\, Nicole Collins\, Lara Felsing\, Braxton
  Garneau\, David Garneau\, Jude Griebel\, Suzy Lake\, Sanaz Mazinani\, Car
 oline Monnet\, nimkii\, Kosisochukwu Nnebe\, Sasha Opeiko\, Naomi Rincón-
 Gallardo\, Michelle Sound\, Kara Springer\, Shaheer Zazai Thursday\, Decem
 ber 3\, 2026 5-9 PM Please join us on Thursday\, December 3\, from 5-9 PM\
 , as we celebrate the opening of Tectonic Shifts . The first exhibition co
 -curated by Contemporary Calgary curators Mona Filip\, Kanika Anand\, and 
 Muriel N. Kahwagi\, Tectonic Shifts invites viewers to consider the subter
 ranean forces that shape\, uphold\, or unsettle inherited structures. Much
  like the deep geological movements the exhibition title evokes\, change i
 s often preceded by imperceptible shifts beneath the surface\, where press
 ures accumulate and fault lines deepen. Straddling social\, political\, em
 otional\, and environmental terrains\, the exhibition foregrounds artists 
 whose works engage notions of disruption\, resistance\, eruption\, and tra
 nsfiguration to envision new pathways from the fragile conditions that hol
 d our worlds together. Rather than treating uncertainty solely as a condit
 ion of crisis\, Tectonic Shifts explores its potential as a generative for
 ce. Through collective defiance\, individual acts of interference\, or mor
 e subtle processes of negotiation and adaptation\, the works consider how 
 moments of upheaval can become catalysts for transformation\, asking how w
 e might engage with\, rather than simply withstand\, the erratic condition
 s of the present. Doors 5:00 PM Remarks 6:30 PM | Atrium Galleries Close 9
 :00 PM This exhibition opening is presented in collaboration with Free Fir
 st Thursday . FREE to the public. No registration is required.
LOCATION:Off Campus #701 11th Street SW Calgary\, AB T2P 2C4
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/exhibition-opening-tectoni
 c-shifts
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Returning to Ourselves | Curatorial Tour with Lisa Deanne Smith
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261202T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261202T183000
UID:returning-ourselves-curatorial-tour-lisa-deanne-smith@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:Returning to Ourselves: Rebecca Baird and Kenny Alvin Baird—
 A Retrospective of Art and Design by Cree Métis Siblings Curatorial Tour 
 with Lisa Deanne Smith\n\nReturning to Ourselves: Rebecca Baird and Kenny 
 Alvin Baird—A Retrospective of Art and Design by Cree Métis Siblings Cu
 ratorial Tour with Lisa Deanne Smith\, Wednesday\, December 02\, 2026\, 5:
 00 p.m. to 6:30 p.m.\, Onsite Gallery\, 199 Richmond Street West\, Toronto
  Join exhibition curator Lisa Deanne Smith for an engaging and in-depth cu
 ratorial tour of Returning to Ourselves: Rebecca Baird and Kenny Alvin Bai
 rd—A Retrospective of Art and Design by Cree Métis Siblings . During th
 is guided experience\, Lisa will share behind-the-scenes insights into the
  exhibition’s development\, exploring the artistic practices\, personal 
 histories\, and creative journeys of siblings Rebecca Baird and Kenny Alvi
 n Baird. This intimate tour offers a unique opportunity to learn more abou
 t the curatorial process\, the relationships that shaped the exhibition\, 
 and the powerful themes woven throughout the artists’ decades-long bodie
 s of work. About the Curator Lisa Deanne Smith is Senior Curator of Onsite
  Gallery at OCAD University. Her curatorial practice examines voice\, embo
 died knowledge\, non-human–centred worldviews\, and the processes throug
 h which knowledge is created and shared. As part of this inquiry\, Smith b
 ecame a certified Forest Therapy guide\, deepening her engagement with lan
 d-based learning. Recent curatorial projects include The Sunshine Eaters \
 , How to Breathe Forever \, power \, and How will we be with you? These mu
 ltidisciplinary exhibitions included works by Mary Anne Barkhouse\, Bonave
 nture Soh Bejeng Ndikung\, Shary Boyle\, Nick Cave\, Rocky Dobey\, John Gr
 eyson\, Brian Jungen\, Jessica Karuhanga\, Natalie King\, Qavavau Manumie\
 , Ekow Nimako\, Alanis Obomsawin\, Ebony G. Patterson\, Rajni Perera\, Pej
 vak\, and Winnie Truong\, among others. About the Exhibition Returning to 
 Ourselves: Rebecca Baird and Kenny Alvin Baird—A Retrospective of Art an
 d Design by Cree Métis Siblings presents over forty years of transformati
 ve work by Rebecca Baird and Kenny Alvin Baird. The exhibition spans sculp
 ture\, painting\, and traditional material practices\, alongside interior 
 design\, film art direction\, graphic design\, and public art commissions.
  Emerging from their Cree Métis histories and ways of knowing\, the Baird
 s approach creative practice through multiple cultural perspectives\, brin
 ging together diverse forms of making grounded in lived experience\, colla
 boration\, and community-centred practice. Visit Registration Page
LOCATION:Onsite Gallery (RHD) 199 Richmond Street West 199 Richmond Street
  West
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/returning-ourselves-curato
 rial-tour-lisa-deanne-smith
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:MAKING IT! series: Dr. Adam Welch
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261201T130020
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261201T143031
UID:making-it-series-dr-adam-welch@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:MAKING IT! Interview Series\n\nMaking It! Arts Professional Ca
 reer Development Series: MAKING IT! In collaboration with Dr. Adam Welch T
 uesday\, December 1\, 1 to 2:30 pm 100 McCaul St.\, 5th floor\, Room 544 P
 ublic welcome Join us for the next instalment of Making It! \, a student-l
 ed conversation series exploring the realities of the professional art wor
 ld and the many paths artists and arts professionals take throughout their
  careers. We are excited to welcome Dr. Adam Welch\, Associate Curator of 
 Modern Art at the Art Gallery of Ontario (AGO). Previously Associate Curat
 or of Canadian Art at the National Gallery of Canada\, Welch has also held
  positions at the Art Museum at the University of Toronto and the Whitney 
 Museum of American Art. He holds a PhD in Art History from the University 
 of Toronto\, where his research examined artistic and curatorial relations
 hips across the Canada–United States border during the 1960s and 1970s. 
 In conversation with Welch\, we will explore the role of curators in the a
 rt world\, how exhibitions and collections come together\, and the relatio
 nships between artists\, institutions\, and the broader cultural landscape
 . We will also discuss how emerging artists can build relationships with c
 urators\, bring their work to the attention of institutions\, and navigate
  opportunities within the art world. This is an opportunity to gain insigh
 t into the curatorial profession and learn more about the many ways of bui
 lding a career in the arts. The Making It! Arts Professional Career Develo
 pment Series is made possible through the Peter Dalglish Career Developmen
 t Series\, generously supported by The Dalglish Family Foundation.
LOCATION:Main Building & Rosalie Sharp Centre for Design (MCA) 100 McCaul 
 Street 100 McCaul St 5th floor\, Room 544
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/making-it-series-dr-adam-w
 elch
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
